00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:54.079
are involved in the producing of food.

00:12:54.079 --> 00:12:59.459
So, most of us, it just shows up in the supermarkets,
it shows up on our plates.

00:12:59.459 --> 00:13:00.459
It's magic.

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:01.620
It's magic.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:06.300
Look, the modern agriculture is nothing short
of miraculous.

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:11.850
But the work that's still done in areas like
artificial insemination and basic animal husbandry

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:14.190
is completely alien to most people.

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:20.970
So the only way you feed 300 million people
three times a day is through a marvel of effectiveness

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:22.180
and efficiency.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:27.700
And, so the modern farmer is heroic and the
modern farmer has an image problem.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:32.190
Because the average person addicted to chewing
and swallowing still sees them as Buck Owens

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:38.150
or Roy Clarke in Hee Haw playin' a banjo and
wearin' overalls and clearin' the south 40.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:46.050
So the same- the same thing that has made
farming difficult, well,

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.350
the military faces it, you know?

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:50.940
One and a half percent of people serve in
the armed forces.

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:57.290
uhm The- the number of tradesmen working right
now is actually very very small

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:00.220
relative to the entire population.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:04.920
And so, we're disconnected, in my opinion,
from things like food, energy,

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.380
the prevailing definition of a good job.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:14.200
uh The most fundamental things are infrastructure,
uh even as we become uber-connected.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:15.620
Literally.

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:16.850
To Uber.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:21.510
Of course I mean it's- it's- it's just beco-
it's fascinating to me

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:24.560
and clearly that's the price.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:30.080
The unintended price we're paying of this
amazing level of connectedness

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:34.000
is this fundamental decoupling from what's really important.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:39.060
Do you think the work ethic suffers when uh
because we're taking for granted all of the

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.790
marvels of the world that, you know, we no
longer live in a world where, if you flip

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:46.440
a switch, the lights don't come on or it shorts
out and shocks you?

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:51.279
The way I would say it is we no longer live
in a world where, when you flick the switch,

00:14:51.279 --> 00:14:55.720
and the lights do come on, your initial feeling
isn't one of wonder.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.510
There is no initial feeling, you know?

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:02.930
The only feeling that comes is when you flick
the switch and lights don't come on.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:06.440
And then isn't it amazing how quickly we're
outraged, you know?

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:09.300
When we flush the toilet and the crap doesn't
go away?

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:10.810
Wha- What?

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:11.810
What?

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:12.810
You know?

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:17.270
The miracle of modern plumbing, the miracle
of affordable electricity, all these things

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:21.740
now we're just not sufficiently gobsmacked
and when we lose our sense of wonder and our

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:28.709
basic appreciation that sets the ground work
for the eventual disconnect that leads to

00:15:28.709 --> 00:15:33.980
vocational education vanishing from high school,
a widening skills gap contemporaneous with

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:38.540
spiking unemployment and all of these seemingly
dichotomous things

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:41.460
that leave people scratching their heads.

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:47.600
My foundation takes the position that these
things aren't problems, these are symptoms

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:55.019
of a larger wrestling and confusion with what
a good job is.

00:15:55.019 --> 00:15:58.250
So talk about the work ethic scholarships
in particular.

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:03.750
Uh what- what uh- what is, what symptom, what
underlying cause is that trying to address?

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:05.550
What symptom is it treating?

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:13.540
I think that, in the same way certain people
believe romantic happiness will only happen

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:18.910
when you locate your soulmate, I think a lot
of people today believe that vocational happiness

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:22.779
will only occur when you find your dream job.

00:16:22.779 --> 00:16:25.230
I th- I think that's nonsense.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:32.029
uhm I also think it's self-defeating because
it just, you know, if you identify the existence

00:16:32.029 --> 00:16:38.390
of the proximate cause of joy and then build
giant barriers between you and it you just

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:40.300
made it very difficult to feel good.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.780
So I think a lot of people have done that
and I think...

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:50.880
I think work ethic suffers as a result because
it's been replaced by this search ethic, right?

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.680
I'm on the search for the thing that will
bring me joy as opposed to

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.760
'no no it doesn't matter what the job is.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:01.920
You wake up early, &nbsp;you stay late, you take
your bite of the crap sandwich, you make yourself

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:08.889
indispensable, you master your job and then
through pluck and insistence and goodwill

00:17:08.889 --> 00:17:12.500
and humor you run the joint in a year and
a half.'

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.460
I still believe that can happen.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:17.380
And on Dirty Jobs I met a lot of people who
proved it.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:24.949
So yeah, there's a bit of earnest Horatio
Alger old man next door 'get off my lawn'

00:17:24.949 --> 00:17:26.750
coming from my foundation.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:30.360
But it's best, I think, embodied in the SWEAT
pledge, you know?

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.360
Which was a product-

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:32.540
And that's an acronym.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.580
So it's not a- it's not a pledge, it's not
like a blood oath, right?

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:36.580
No.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.780
No, it stands for Skills and Work Ethic Aren't
Taboo.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:49.130
And it was the product of a six pack of Lagunitas
and careful reflection on my part on two things:

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:50.130
the boy scout law

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:52.200
And you are an Eagle scout?

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:53.200
Yeah.

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:57.840
And the uh, the twelve step recovery process
from Alcoholics Anonymous.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:06.460
And I'm- and I'm not a member of AA but I
love the idea that there's a twelve step process

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:13.010
that might be applied to ideas of personal
responsibility and, and personal accountability

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:13.630
and work ethic.

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:17.680
And you guys have, at this point, you've given
out something like four million dollars and

00:18:17.690 --> 00:18:20.960
what- what does that money go to? and where
do you get it from?

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:26.510
The money comes primarily from me auctioning
off the crap in my garage.

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.549
And crap is also an acronym?

00:18:28.549 --> 00:18:29.799
-also and acronym.

00:18:29.799 --> 00:18:32.400
It's important to always have acronyms, Nick.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.230
CRAP stands for Collectables Rare and Precious.

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:38.539
So the idea was, my garage is just full of
stuff.

00:18:38.539 --> 00:18:43.620
Filthy mementoes from my misspent career and
I take these mementoes and I autograph them

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.730
and I try and tell an amusing story.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:52.330
And in the same way Jerry Luis would hope
to uh guilt you to giving money during a telethon

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:56.391
by some sort of performance, I aim to raise
money by entertaining you with a tale.

00:18:56.391 --> 00:19:00.380
Talk- what is the uh most disgusting item
that you've auctioned off?

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.100
Well, depending on who you ask I just got
sixteen grand

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:05.240
for an autographed Donald Trump bathrobe.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.250
I was going to bring that up.

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:07.500
But, so there's that.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.290
But then talk about the semen.

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:15.430
Okay. uhm, I collected uh semen uh I've collected
semen from many many many many many creatures.

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:16.462
Well, somebody's gotta do it, right?

00:19:16.462 --> 00:19:17.559
It has to be done.

