00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:38.120
You- you're not going to endorse anybody for
president but how do you see the politics

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:41.370
of the moment this is, you know, the craziest
election.

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:45.900
Do you worry about the direction of the country
beyond these questions about work ethic and

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:50.170
the mismatch between jobs, available jobs,
and training for jobs.

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:54.309
uhm Bernie or rather Hillary Clinton and Donald
Trump are both very protectionist.

00:23:54.309 --> 00:23:55.309
Yep

00:23:55.309 --> 00:23:57.470
They talk a lot about how trade deals are
bad.

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:04.110
How does that play into your world view about
trad- or jobs that are here and whatnot as-

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:09.110
I mean, I have a personal opinion on it but
the honest, and I think better, answer is

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:11.620
'How do you want to set the table?'

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:17.690
Are we going to set the table in a way that
fundamentally advantages the American consumer?

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:22.750
or are we gonna set it in a way that fundamentally
advantages the American worker?

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:27.210
And it's not a rhetorical question, it's already
been asked and we've already answered it.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:31.740
We've set the table in a way that benefits
the consumer and I think we've done it because

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:33.789
there are more consumers than workers.

00:24:33.789 --> 00:24:36.460
All workers are consumers, right?

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:37.700
Not all consumers are workers.

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:42.520
So we're in the global economy, you must be
this tall to get on the ride and there's no

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:43.520
negotiating.

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:45.220
We're in it right now.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:53.870
So, you know, that's the bargain we've made
and we're right now realizing that maybe,

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:58.470
you know, maybe that wasn't the table we wanted
to set after all.

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:02.830
But I don't know how you put the poop back
in the goose, you know? we either want to

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:07.340
buy our Fords for as little money as possible-

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:09.610
Then again another product placement, right?

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:11.470
I'm done with Ford.

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:12.470
They're done with me.

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.679
But it might come back around so I'm not gonna
say anything too nasty.

00:25:15.679 --> 00:25:20.010
No, look I- I've- I've driven Fords all my
life and I was proud to speak on their behalf

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:27.190
for a few years but, you know, when Donald
says he's gonna charge 35% more for a Ford

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:30.320
that's made in Mexico the crowd goes wild.

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:35.440
And, I get it, it sounds pretty good, let's
level stuff out, why not?

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:40.650
But that does mean that we're gonna be paying
35% more either because it's tax coming in

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:43.490
from Mexico or it costs much more to make
here.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:47.960
The brain tends to solve the problem it looks
at.

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:56.549
And when we're just looking at how to better
manufacturing for workers, obviously- obviously

00:25:56.549 --> 00:26:00.990
raise those tariffs, get those factories back
here, problem solved.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:03.669
But there's always an unintended consequence.

00:26:03.669 --> 00:26:08.640
It's the same kind of thinking that says 'look
we all want to see people make a living wage,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:12.750
we all want to see people make ends meet,
so let's just raise that minimum wage.

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:13.750
Boom!

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:15.290
Problem solved.

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:19.929
Nobody wants to see people thrown out of their
apartment, nobody wants to see hard working

00:26:19.929 --> 00:26:22.450
people displaced so let's control the rent.

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:27.390
You're in your fifties, you remember uh you
know both because of your personal story but

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:31.770
just 'cause of the time you lived in, you
remember periods where unemployment was double

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:36.010
digits, uh, you know where inflation was out
of control, where the economy looked pretty

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:37.010
bleak.

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:41.610
uhm Do you think millennials are facing anything
like you might have faced in your, you know,

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:45.659
in your teen years, or what the world looked
like or when you were their age.

00:26:45.659 --> 00:26:51.400
What- you know, do they have a future? and
is it better than when you grew up in? or

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:53.980
what has to happen to kinda make things better?

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:57.270
Yeah that's a- that's a great macro question.

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:05.390
uhm My answer is micro, you know, uhm my foundation's
called Mike Rowe Works because I really, I'm

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:08.340
not smart enough to speak that holistically.

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:16.779
But I take great comfort in finding an individual
who is willing to look at the landscape however

00:27:16.779 --> 00:27:20.200
bleak and see opportunity.

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:26.380
And I just think that that's always been for
sale in good times and bad and the first time,

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:31.549
well not the first time, but the first time
as an adult that I- that I was really confronted

00:27:31.549 --> 00:27:39.080
by that was in late 2008 on Dirty Jobs when
the headlines all across the country were

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:44.820
screaming double digit unemployment and every
major news story and every major news cast

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:51.470
started with the number of people out of work
and everywhere I went on the show, Nick, every-

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:54.820
every state, all I saw were help wanted signs.

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:59.750
And so, there was this other narrative going
on in the country that had to do with the

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:05.480
inability to find people who were willing
to learn a skill that was actually in demand

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:09.770
and I was fascinated by how little that got
reported.

00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:14.400
So 2008 the skills gap was officially 2.3
million jobs, today it's 5.8 million.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:19.659
And that means that these are jobs that are
available, you know, that nobody's applying

00:28:19.659 --> 00:28:21.080
for or nobody's qualified for.

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:23.190
Where nobody's getting hired for, right?

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:26.370
It does- it gets political and I never mean
it to.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:33.580
But it- it's impossible to not wind up in
a political conversation when one side is

00:28:33.580 --> 00:28:38.250
saying 'look at all these people who aren't
working' and the other side is saying 'look

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.720
at all these jobs we can't fill.'

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:43.980
A reasonable person has to say 'Why?

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.240
What, What's the problem?

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:53.270
Is it really a lack of skill? could it be
a lack of will? is it uh is it a geography

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:54.270
problem?

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:55.270
Is it really a training problem?

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:56.270
Is it a money problem?

00:28:56.270 --> 00:28:58.010
Is it an education problem?'

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:04.149
And just, you know, the left will tell you
that the reason these jobs are unfilled is

00:29:04.149 --> 00:29:08.429
because these greedy rapacious capitalists
simply aren't paying enough and the right

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:11.929
will tell you the reason these jobs aren't
filled is because these lazy good for nothing

00:29:11.929 --> 00:29:19.940
lay-abouts just don't have the work ethic
required, or the ambition uh or perhaps we're

00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:24.000
not gonna let 'em starve, we're alrea- we're
gonna take care of them, you know, they're

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:27.580
making fouty, fourty five thousand dollars
a year not working right now in total benefits

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:30.030
so, and so now you're in a polemic, right?

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:34.950
And now we're talking about the human condition
instead of an opportunity.

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:37.549
So it gets, it gets pretty wonky pretty quick.

00:29:37.549 --> 00:29:41.980
And that's what your- I mean your foundation
exists to kind of match up people who want

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:44.370
jobs to figure out how to get there.

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:49.010
To get to the place both literally and figuratively
where the jobs are.

00:29:49.010 --> 00:29:50.350
